# Long Live Tindivanam

Tindivanam has taught a good lesson to the northerners and to those who trail behind them holding their tails.

Dravidam praises Tindivanam's courage. At the same time, those who regard the very name Dravidam as an incurable poison tremble at the mention of Tindivanam.

In the parliamentary election for the Tindivanam constituency, Congress had fielded Goenka, a north Indian Bania, a great moneybag, and a capitalist who exploits Dravidam through the newspaper business.

We condemned it at the time. All the discerning people of the country stood on our side.

The pure Tamil scholar Tirukkural Munusamy opposed the Bania. The *Tirukkural* clashed with the iron cashbox!

Beside the bullock-box stood the Bania; beside the rooster-box stood the warrior sounding the drum of Dravidam's ideal.

It was the rooster that crowed; the bull fled in bewilderment!

Goenka waited, thinking the election would be a sweet clove fruit. When it burst, he fled in fear.

The rooster's crow is heard throughout India today. That crow echoes: Tindivanam is a city of heroes.

We commend the heroes of Tindivanam. We regard Tirukkural Munusamy's victory as a victory for the cause of a sovereign Dravidian state, and we congratulate him. Let us also thank the defeated Goenka for helping to measure the mind of the Dravidian people!

(*Dravida Nadu*, news item, 27-1-02)
